Certainly! Below is an optimized JSX script for Adobe InDesign that transforms a spreads document (two-page spreads) into a single-page document while maintaining the original margins, layout, positioning of elements, and references to master pages.

```jsx
#target indesign

// Function to convert spreads to single pages
function convertSpreadsToSinglePages(doc) {
    if (!doc || !doc.pages.length) {
        alert("No document or pages found.");
        return;
    }

    // Create a new document to hold the single pages
    var newDoc = app.documents.add();
    newDoc.documentPreferences = doc.documentPreferences.duplicate();

    // Iterate through all spreads in the original document
    for (var i = 0; i < doc.spreads.length; i++) {
        var spread = doc.spreads[i];

        // Iterate through all pages in the current spread
        for (var j = 0; j < spread.pages.length; j++) {
            var page = spread.pages[j];

            // Create a new page in the new document
            var newPage = newDoc.pages.add();

            // Copy the page from the original document to the new document
            var items = page.pageItems.everyItem().getElements();
            for (var k = 0; k < items.length; k++) {
                var item = items[k];
                var newItem = item.duplicate(LocationOptions.AFTER, newPage);
                newItem.geometricBounds = item.geometricBounds;
            }

            // Set the master page for the new page
            if (page.appliedMaster) {
                newPage.appliedMaster = page.appliedMaster.duplicate();
            }
        }
    }

    // Close the original document
    doc.close(SaveOptions.DONOTSAVECHANGES);

    alert("Spreads have been converted to single pages in the new document.");
}

// Main function to run the script
function main() {
    var doc = app.activeDocument;
    if (doc) {
        convertSpreadsToSinglePages(doc);
    } else {
        alert("No active document found.");
    }
}

// Run the main function
main();
```

### Explanation:
1. **Function `convertSpreadsToSinglePages(doc)`**:
   - Checks if the document and its pages exist.
   - Creates a new document with the same preferences as the original.
   - Iterates through each spread and then each page within the spread.
   - For each page, creates a new page in the new document and duplicates all page items, maintaining their geometric bounds.
   - Sets the master page for the new page if the original page had one applied.
   - Closes the original document without saving changes.

2. **Function `main()`**:
   - Retrieves the active document.
   - Calls `convertSpreadsToSinglePages` with the active document if it exists.

3. **Running the Script**:
   - The script is executed when the `main()` function is called.

This script should be saved with a `.jsx` extension and run within Adobe InDesign. It ensures that all elements, margins, and master page references are preserved while converting the document from spreads to single pages.

